reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
The only random draw is the pot (6-14k). It applies equally to all sharers in a round, so it shifts stakes, not who wins. Its effect is weighting: an elimination or duck in a 14k round matters more, which estimates chance at well under 15% of the result.
There is no random deal. All hands start identical at 3 Bang and 5 Click.
The outcome is decided by:
- inferring each rival's remaining Bangs from revealed shots and from when they could have burned cards face down;
- reading public targets before deciding to Stand or Duck;
- bluffing with Clicks;
- planning when to spend Bangs over 8 rounds;
- coalition talk about who to focus.
With all players playing well, the result comes from mixed-strategy guesses and inference, not from a draw. A reasoner who tracks Bang counts and avoids predictability is clearly favoured over a random or fixed-policy player, as the policy tests above show.
